<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demosthenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">384–<a href="/years/322-bce">322 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demosthenes was an Athenian statesman and the greatest orator of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In speeches such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olynthiacs">Olynthiacs</a> and Philippics he urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, and he helped build the alliance defeated at Chaeronea in 338 BCE. After a failed revolt against Macedonian rule in 322 BCE he took poison to avoid capture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/demosthenes.jpg" alt="Demosthenes" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Polyeuctus · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Demosthenes_Polyeuctos_Louvre_Ma237_n01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Demosthenes was born in 384 BCE, the son of a wealthy sword-maker, also named Demosthenes, of the deme of Paeania. His rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschines">Aeschines</a> claimed his mother Kleoboule was Scythian by blood, an allegation some modern scholars dispute. He was orphaned at seven; his guardians Aphobus, Demophon and Therippides mishandled his inheritance. He began to learn rhetoric because he wished to take them to court; being of delicate physique, he could not take the usual gymnastic education. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> says he overcame a stammering pronunciation by speaking with pebbles in his mouth and practised before a large mirror. At twenty he delivered his first judicial speeches, successfully claiming what remained of his inheritance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He then made his living as a logographer, writing speeches for use in private suits, and as an advocate, and he stopped advocacy once he entered politics. He first spoke publicly on politics in 354 BCE. In 357 BCE he was among the first volunteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trierarchy">trierarchs</a>, and in 348 BCE he paid for a theatrical production as a choregos. Between 355 and 351 BCE he wrote prosecutions such as Against Androtion and Against Leptines, which show his principles on foreign policy: the importance of the navy, of alliances and of national honour.</p><p>Most of his major orations opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>, who had seized Amphipolis and Pydna in 357 BCE. In 352 BCE he called Philip the worst enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and in 351 BCE, in the First Philippic, he told the Athenians that if anything happened to Philip they would soon raise up a second one. After Chaeronea Philip made peace with Athens on lenient terms, and Demosthenes was chosen to deliver the Funeral Oration. When Philip was assassinated in 336 BCE, Demosthenes rejoiced and played a leading part in the uprising of Athens against the new king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Alexander moved swiftly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, which submitted, and when the Athenians begged mercy he admonished them but imposed no punishment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his efforts against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> failed, the revolt met a harsh reaction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> sent men to track him down, and Demosthenes killed himself to avoid arrest by Archias of Thurii. He was among the Athenian politicians who published their speeches, which survived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. Sixty-one orations attributed to him survive, though some are pseudonymous. The Alexandrian Canon listed him among the ten great Attic orators, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> praised him as the perfect orator.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Demosthenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Demosthenes</b> claimed in 355 BCE that the Bosporus sent Athens about 400,000 medimnoi of grain a year, as much as all its other sources combined; the figure is debated, but the trade was clearly vital to Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>By asking Philip II to crush the Phocians it brought Macedonian power close to its borders, and in 338 BCE, persuaded by <b>Demosthenes</b>, it joined Athens and lost at Chaeronea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…the tragedies of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides and the comedies of Aristophanes; history, invented as a form of inquiry by Herodotus and refined by Thucydides; the speeches of <b>Demosthenes</b>; and philosophy, from the dialogues of Plato to the treatises of Aristotle, which covered almost every field of knowledge.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>In 5th-century BCE accounts the building is simply called “the temple”; the name Parthenon, linked to the statue of Athena Parthenos, first certainly refers to the whole building in the 4th-century BCE orator <b>Demosthenes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite <b>Demosthenes</b>' Olynthiac speeches urging Athenian help, Philip took the city by treachery in 348 BCE, razed it and sold its people into slavery.</blockquote><a href="/state/olynthus">Olynthus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Upper Paiania, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Poros, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.84&amp;year=-344" aria-label="Upper Paiania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5144 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Demosthenes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5594 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5594 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.84&amp;year=-344">What was here in 344 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is impossible, men of Athens, utterly impossible, to acquire power that will last, by unrighteousness, by perjury, and by falsehood.&#8221;</p><footer>Second Olynthiac &#183; tr. A. W. Pickard-Cambridge, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9060/pg9060-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do you want to go round asking one another, 'Is there any news?' Could there be any stranger news than that a man of Macedonia is defeating Athenians in war, and ordering the affairs of the Hellenes?&#8221;</p><footer>First Philippic &#183; tr. A. W. Pickard-Cambridge, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9060/pg9060-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it cannot be that you were wrong, men of Athens, when you took upon you the struggle for freedom and deliverance. No! by those who at Marathon bore the brunt of the peril—our forefathers.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Crown &#183; tr. A. W. Pickard-Cambridge, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9061/pg9061-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You, men of Athens, have the greatest power in the world-warships, infantry, cavalry, revenue. But none of these elements of power have you used as you ought, down to this very day.&#8221;</p><footer>First Philippic &#183; tr. A. W. Pickard-Cambridge, 1912 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9060/pg9060-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is empire that he desires, and you, as he believes, are his only possible rivals in this.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Chersonese &#183; tr. A. W. Pickard-Cambridge, 1912 &#183; of Philip II of Macedon &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9060/pg9060-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
